# New publication explores the neutron skin of calcium-48 at the LHC

**Published 18 May 2026**

A new open-access article published in *The European Physical Journal C* investigates how collisions at CERN’s Large Hadron Collider could be used to study the neutron skin of calcium-48.

The neutron skin describes the difference between the distributions of neutrons and protons inside an atomic nucleus. In the new study, the researchers use the AMPT simulation model to compare collisions of calcium-40 nuclei with collisions of calcium-48 nuclei at a centre-of-mass energy of 5.02 TeV per nucleon pair.

The study examines whether the thickness of the neutron skin in calcium-48 leaves a measurable imprint on collective flow—the coordinated motion of particles produced in high-energy nuclear collisions. Particular attention is given to anisotropic flow coefficients and event-by-event fluctuations in the particles’ mean transverse momentum.

The simulations indicate that triangular flow, quadrangular flow, and certain measures of transverse-momentum fluctuations are sensitive to changes in the neutron-skin thickness. The authors therefore conclude that future calcium-isotope collision runs at the LHC could provide an independent and complementary method for constraining the neutron skin of calcium-48.

The article was based on the master's thesis of Andreas Vitsos and the bachelor's thesis of Leonora Misciattelli Mocenigo Soranzo, both supervised by You Zhou from the University of Copenhagen. 
The author list includes the aforementioned Andreas Vitsos, Leonora Misciattelli Mocenigo Soranzo, You Zhou and Emil Gorm Dahlbæk Nielsen.

